---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Add Verdict to your browser"
  description="Install Verdict for Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, or Firefox."
>
  <h1>Add Verdict to your browser</h1>
  <p>
    It takes about thirty seconds. Verdict asks for access to shopping sites only, and for
    local storage to keep your history on your machine. It does not ask for anything else.
  </p>

  <section id="chromium" data-browser-block="chromium">
    <h2>Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Arc and other Chromium browsers</h2>
    <p>One store listing covers all of them.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Open the Verdict listing on the Chrome Web Store.</li>
      <li>Choose Add to Chrome, then Add extension.</li>
      <li>
        Pin Verdict to your toolbar so you can reach your history. Click the puzzle piece icon
        in the toolbar, find Verdict, and click the pin.
      </li>
      <li>Open any Amazon product page. Verdict appears under the star rating within a second or two.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Edge, Brave, Opera and Arc all install Chrome Web Store extensions directly. Edge may ask
      you to allow extensions from other stores the first time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="firefox" data-browser-block="firefox">
    <h2>Firefox</h2>
    <p>
      Verdict is on addons.mozilla.org as a signed extension. Choose Add to Firefox, then Add.
      If you used the Review Checker that Firefox removed in June 2025, this is the closest
      replacement, and it does more.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="safari">
    <h2>Safari</h2>
    <p>
      Not yet. Safari requires a different packaging route and a paid developer account. It is
      on the list, without a date.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="development-build">
    <h2>Installing the development build yourself</h2>
    <p>For people who would rather read the code and build it.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Download the latest <code>verdict-chrome.zip</code> from the releases page, or build it
        with <code>just ext build</code>.
      </li>
      <li>Unzip it somewhere you will not delete by accident.</li>
      <li>Open <code>chrome://extensions</code> and turn on Developer mode, top right.</li>
      <li>Choose Load unpacked and select the unzipped folder.</li>
      <li>Chrome will warn you about developer mode extensions on every startup. That is normal.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The build is reproducible. Every release lists the commit it was built from and the
      SHA256 of the zip, so you can check that what is in the store matches what is in the
      repository.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="checking">
    <h2>Checking it works, and removing it</h2>
    <p>
      Open any Amazon product page with a few hundred reviews. Verdict appears under the
      rating. If nothing happens, the <a href="/status">status page</a> shows whether extraction
      is currently working for your country.
    </p>
    <p>
      To remove it: right click the Verdict icon and choose Remove from Chrome. Your history is
      stored inside the extension, so removing it deletes the history too. Export first if you
      want to keep it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <script>
    // progressive enhancement only: every section above is already in the
    // page and readable without javascript, this just reorders the matching
    // block to the top so it is the first thing a visitor sees
    const isFirefox = navigator.userAgent.includes("Firefox");
    const matchId = isFirefox ? "firefox" : "chromium";
    const match = document.getElementById(matchId);
    if (match !== null && match.parentElement !== null) {
      match.parentElement.prepend(match);
    }
  </script>
</Layout>
